WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.931
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.931 --> 00:00:02.727
Wednesday the twenty-first.

00:00:02.727 --> 00:00:06.984
Gym at seven, Godinger till five, an appointment at five-thirty.

00:00:06.984 --> 00:00:09.378
Quick one to chew on while you move.

00:00:09.378 --> 00:00:13.169
What year did the railroads put America on standard time?

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:14.499
Answer's at the end.

00:00:14.499 --> 00:00:16.162
Weather's dry and bright.

00:00:16.162 --> 00:00:19.820
Sunny all day, high in the low sixties, no rain at all.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.549
The thing to know is wind.

00:00:21.549 --> 00:00:27.070
It's out of the northeast, ten to fifteen miles an hour, with gusts up near thirty.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:32.656
Riding in to work, that's mostly in your face, so give yourself a few extra minutes.

00:00:32.656 --> 00:00:34.652
Coming home it's at your back.

00:00:34.652 --> 00:00:39.307
Tonight it swings north, stays breezy, and drops to the upper forties.

00:00:39.307 --> 00:00:44.096
This is from yesterday afternoon's forecast, so it could shift a little.

00:00:44.096 --> 00:00:45.027
Okay, the day.

00:00:45.027 --> 00:00:45.892
Gym at seven.

00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:47.555
It's straight sets today.

00:00:47.555 --> 00:00:50.814
Compounds to one rep in reserve, squat and R.D.L.

00:00:50.814 --> 00:00:51.279
to two.

00:00:51.279 --> 00:00:55.070
Save the myo-reps for lateral raises and rear-delt flies.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.199
Then Godinger from nine to five.

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:01.921
Right after that, an appointment at five-thirty, and it's a video call.

00:01:01.921 --> 00:01:03.717
So five-thirty, video call.

00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:12.629
That's tight if you're still on the bike at five-twenty, so either head out a bit early or find a quiet corner and take it from there.

00:01:12.629 --> 00:01:16.486
It wraps at six-thirty, and after that the night is yours.

00:01:16.486 --> 00:01:17.218
Okay, news.

00:01:17.218 --> 00:01:20.211
First, Anthropic put out a draft federal A.I.

00:01:20.211 --> 00:01:21.009
safety bill.

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:29.456
The idea is that the government could ask a federal judge to pause a model's release if it poses an imminent risk of mass harm.

00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:34.112
The bar is more than fifty deaths or over a billion dollars in damage.

00:01:34.112 --> 00:01:37.304
The Commerce Department would bring those cases.

00:01:37.304 --> 00:01:44.421
It's a narrow trigger on purpose, and it's interesting to see a lab write the rules it wants to be held to.

00:01:44.421 --> 00:01:48.478
Second, those cost of living protests in Europe keep growing.

00:01:48.478 --> 00:01:53.333
France says about five hundred fifty thousand people marched on Saturday.

00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:58.188
Across the unrest, there have been more than twenty-four hundred arrests.

00:01:58.188 --> 00:02:05.371
And in Spain, the prime minister just called an early election after parliament voted down his housing plan.

00:02:05.371 --> 00:02:09.961
Housing costs are now toppling governments, not just filling streets.

00:02:09.961 --> 00:02:11.424
Third, closer to home.

00:02:11.424 --> 00:02:20.270
The mayor plans to expand the city's community violence prevention program into three new neighborhoods, and Bushwick is one of them.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:27.719
That's about five million dollars, already budgeted, going to local groups that step in before shootings happen.

00:02:27.719 --> 00:02:32.308
So you may start seeing those outreach teams around the neighborhood.

00:02:32.308 --> 00:02:35.700
Around you tonight, two good shows in Williamsburg.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.427
Yaya Bey is at Baby's All Right at seven.

00:02:38.427 --> 00:02:44.213
If you want something louder, Built to Spill plays Music Hall of Williamsburg at eight.

00:02:44.213 --> 00:02:47.871
Either one is an easy ride after your six-thirty wraps.

00:02:47.871 --> 00:02:50.997
I couldn't find prices, so check before you go.

00:02:50.997 --> 00:02:53.325
Today's challenge is for your head.

00:02:53.325 --> 00:02:54.921
Eat lunch with no phone.

00:02:54.921 --> 00:02:58.047
Not in your pocket, not face down on the table.

00:02:58.047 --> 00:02:59.045
Somewhere else.

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:00.708
Just eat and look around.

00:03:00.708 --> 00:03:05.563
When you're done, name three things you noticed that you'd normally miss.

00:03:05.563 --> 00:03:08.689
A sound, a smell, how the light hits the floor.

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:10.485
It's maybe fifteen minutes.

00:03:10.485 --> 00:03:14.675
It'll feel weirdly long at first, and that's kind of the point.

00:03:14.675 --> 00:03:17.335
Alright, something you've been learning.

00:03:17.335 --> 00:03:21.392
Remember picking the memo's voice off that arena leaderboard?

00:03:21.392 --> 00:03:23.454
Here's how those rankings work.

00:03:23.454 --> 00:03:24.186
It's blind.

00:03:24.186 --> 00:03:29.506
You hear two voices read the same line, and you don't know which model is which.

00:03:29.506 --> 00:03:32.034
You just pick the one you like better.

00:03:32.034 --> 00:03:37.687
That one vote is a tiny match, and every model carries a rating, like a chess player.

00:03:37.687 --> 00:03:39.017
That's the Elo part.

00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:44.471
Before each match, the system guesses who should win, based on the gap in ratings.

00:03:44.471 --> 00:03:47.597
If the favorite wins, both ratings barely move.

00:03:47.597 --> 00:03:49.459
Nothing surprising happened.

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:55.379
If the underdog wins, the ratings swing a lot, because the system just learned something.

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:57.308
The gap itself has a meaning.

00:03:57.308 --> 00:04:03.427
A hundred points apart means the higher one should win about sixty-four percent of the time.

00:04:03.427 --> 00:04:06.486
Four hundred points apart is about ten to one.

00:04:06.486 --> 00:04:08.747
Here's the part worth remembering.

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:11.275
Near the top, the gaps are often tiny.

00:04:11.275 --> 00:04:14.999
A voice ten or twenty points ahead isn't clearly better.

00:04:14.999 --> 00:04:19.921
It'd win barely more than half the time, and that's well inside the noise.

00:04:19.921 --> 00:04:24.510
So when the top few are bunched together, any of them is a fair pick.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.636
After that, it's your ear, not the leaderboard.

00:04:27.636 --> 00:04:29.498
And the answer from the top.

00:04:29.498 --> 00:04:36.948
The railroads switched to standard time in eighteen eighty-three, on the day people called the day of two noons.

00:04:36.948 --> 00:04:39.941
It didn't become law until nineteen eighteen.

00:04:39.941 --> 00:04:43.000
Have a good lift, and enjoy the tailwind home.
